1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Liu Shuo 劉鑠 (431–453), ''zi'' xiuxuan 休玄, Liu-Song period poet
2 CONT
2 CONT Liu Shuo’s ancestral home was Pengcheng 彭城 (modern Xuzhou 徐州, Jiangsu). He was the fourth son of Emperor Wen (r. 424–423) of the Song dynasty. In 439, he was named Prince of Nanping 南平 and in the following year was appointed regio
2 CONC nal inspector of Xiangzhou 湘州 (administrative seat Linxiang 臨湘, modern Changsha, Hunan). However, given his young age he did not assume this official post, but was assigned to the garrison in Shitou cheng 石頭城 (modern Qingliang sha
2 CONC n 清涼山, Nanjing). In 445, he was appointed regional inspector of Nanyuzhou 南豫州 (administrative seat Liyang 歷陽, modern He 和 county, Anhui) and general of the right army. During the northern expedition against the Wei in 450, Liu Shu
2 CONC o took command of an army led by the generals Hu Shengzhi 胡盛之 and Dao Tanzhi 到坦之. The following year he was stationed at Shitou cheng with the title of General of the Pacification Army. In 453, in a palace coup, Liu Shuo’s eldest 
2 CONC brother Liu Shao 劉劭 (425–453) murdered their father Emperor Wen and attempted to establish himself on the imperial throne. Liu Shuo allied with Liu Shao and was promoted to general of middle army. He later served as regional inspe
2 CONC ctor of Nanyanzhou 南袞州 (administrative seat Guangling 廣陵, modern Yangzhou). When Liu Shuo’s brother Liu Jun 劉駿 (430–464) launched a successful coup against his brother Liu Shao and established himself as emperor (Emperor Xiaowu 孝武
2 CONC 帝, r. 454–464), he appointed Liu Shuo palace attendant and minister of works. But shortly after he assumed office, Liu Shuo died of poisoning at the age of twenty-three.
2 CONT
2 CONT Liu Shuo displayed unusual intelligence from an early age. He was widely read and was a gifted writer. By the age of twenty he had composed thirty-plus imitations of ''gushi'' 古詩 (anonymous pentasyllabic poems). His contemporaries
2 CONC  thought his verse rivaled that of Lu Ji 陸機 (261–303). Two of his imitations of “Nineteen Old Poems” #1 and 19 are included in the ''Wen xuan'' 文選. He also has five poems in the ''Yutai xinyong''. The ''Shi pin'' 詩品 ranks his vers
2 CONC e in the “lower grade. ” His “Shuixian fu” 水仙賦 (''Fu'' on river sprite), which is no longer extant, was considered the equal of the “Luoshen fu” 洛神賦 (''Fu'' on the Luo River goddess) of Cao Zhi. Liu Shuo was also a good musician. 
2 CONC While he was serving at Nanyanzhou, he composed “Shouyang yue” 壽陽樂 (Music of Shouyang) based on local folk songs.
2 CONT
2 CONT The monographs on bibliography of the ''Sui shu'' and the two Tang histories list Liu Shuo’s collected works in five ''juan''. This was lost in the Song. Lu Qinli has collected ten of his poems in ''Xian Qin Han Wei Jin Nanbeichao
2 CONC  shi'', and Yan Kejun has collected his only extant prose work in ''Quan Shanggu Sandai Qin Han Sanguo Liuchao wen''.
2 CONT
2 CONT Not Specified Taiping Chang David R. Knechtges
2 CONT
2 CONT Citation
