0 @N6000000002188193921@ NOTE Emperor Fei of Western Wei
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT (Xi) Wei Feidi ((?)???)
1 CONT Family name: Yuan (?, yuán)
1 CONT Given name: Qin (?, qin)
1 CONT Posthumous name: None
1 CONT
1 CONT Emperor Fei of Western Wei ((?)???) (d. 554), personal name Yuan Qin (??), was an emperor of the Chinese/Xianbei state Western Wei -- a branch successor state of Northern Wei. He, even more so than his father Emperor Wen, held lit
1 CONC tle actual power in the face of overwhelming control of power by the paramount general Yuwen Tai. In 554, he tried to plot to have Yuwen killed, but his plot was discovered, and Yuwen deposed him, and soon had him killed.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] As crown prince
1 CONT
1 CONT It is not known when Yuan Qin was born. What is known is that he was the oldest son of Yuan Baoju the Prince of Nanyang, a grandson of Emperor Xiaowen of Northern Wei, by his wife Princess Yifu, and that he had one younger brother
1 CONC  by Princess Yifu, Yuan Wu (??). Around the new year 535, Emperor Xiaowu of Northern Wei -- a cousin of Yuan Baoju and the final emperor of the undivided Northern Wei state but who by now was only emperor over the western part of 
1 CONC the state (i.e., Western Wei) -- was poisoned by the paramount general Yuwen Tai over a dispute developed when Yuwen disapproved of his incestuous relationship with Yuan Baoju's sister Yuan Mingyue (???). Yuwen Tai made Yuan Baoju
1 CONC  the new emperor (as Emperor Wen). Emperor Wen created Princess Yifu empress, and created Yuan Qin crown prince. In 538, while both Emperor Wen and Yuwen Tai were away on a campaign against Eastern Wei, Yuan Qin was nominally put 
1 CONC in charge of the capital Chang'an, although the official Zhou Huida (???) was actually in charge. When former Eastern Wei captives, upon hearing initial news of Eastern Wei victories, rebelled within Chang'an with Zhao Qingque (??
1 CONC ?), Zhou and Li Hu (??) were forced to escort the crown prince out of Chang'an to flee the rebellion. Eventually, when Yuwen returned, Zhao's rebellion collapsed, and Emperor Wen and Yuan Qin were both able to return to Chang'an.
1 CONT
1 CONT Emperor Wen had little actual power, and also in 538, over Yuwen's desire for an alliance with Rouran, he was forced to depose Empress Yifu and marry the daughter of Rouran's Chiliantoubingdoufa Khan Yujiulü Anagui as his empress.
1 CONC  In 540, he was further forced to initially send Empress Yujiulü away from Chang'an (to live with Yuan Qin's younger brother Yuan Wu), and then forced to order her to commit suicide.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sometime during Emperor Wen's reign, Yuan Qin married Yuwen Tai's daughter as his crown princess. It was said that he favored her greatly, and that he had no concubines.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 542, during an assault that Eastern Wei's paramount general Gao Huan launched on the border city Yubi (??, in modern Yuncheng, Shanxi), Yuan Qin was made the defender of the important city Puban (??, in modern Yuncheng as well)
1 CONC , while Yuwen launched an army to try to catch up with Gao as he retreated. It is not known how much responsibility or authority Yuan Qin actually had during this campaign.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 551, Emperor Wen died. Yuan Qin took the throne (as Emperor Fei).
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] As emperor
1 CONT
1 CONT Emperor Fei appeared to have even less actual power than his father, to whom Yuwen Tai paid formal deference. He was able to rebury his mother Empress Yifu with his father Emperor Wen. He created his wife, Crown Princess Yuwen, em
1 CONC press. Apparently because Yuwen wanted to restore many Zhou Dynasty customs and traditions, Emperor Fei did not use an era name; rather, the years of his reign were merely referred to as "the first year," "the second year," and so
1 CONC  on.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 553, Yuwen Tai's nephew Weichi Jiong conquered the western provinces of Liang Dynasty, which had been previously under the control of the Liang pretender to the throne, Xiao Ji, and added them to Western Wei territory.
1 CONT
1 CONT In late 553, the official Yuan Lie (??) plotted killing Yuwen, but his plot was discovered, and Yuwen killed him. After Yuan Lie's death, Emperor Fei became angry at Yuwen and often spoke against him. He also secretly plotted kill
1 CONC ing Yuwen, despite advice from Yuan Yu (??) the Prince of Linhuai and Yuan Zan (??, the nephew of Emperor Xiaowu) the Prince of Guangping that plotting was dangerous. Emperor Fei's plot, however, was eventually discovered by Yuwen
1 CONC 's other sons-in-law. Yuwen deposed and imprisoned Emperor Fei, making his younger brother Yuan Kuo emperor (as Emperor Gong). Soon thereafter, Emperor Fei was put to death. According to the History of Northern Dynasties, his wife
1 CONC  Empress Yuwen "also suffered death because she was faithful to Wei's imperial house," although it is unclear exactly how she died or whether she died the same year he did.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Personal information
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Father
1 CONT           o Emperor Wen of Western Wei
1 CONT     * Mother
1 CONT           o Empress Yifu
1 CONT     * Wife
1 CONT           o Empress Yuwen (created 551?, d. 554?)
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Emperor Wen of Western Wei Emperor of Northern Wei (Western)
1 CONT 551-554 Succeeded by
1 CONT Emperor Gong of Western Wei
1 CONT Emperor of China (Western)
1 CONT 551-554
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Xiao Ji of Liang Emperor of China (Southwestern)
1 CONT 553-554
0 @N6000000002188193948@ NOTE Emperor Gong of Western Wei
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT (Xi) Wei Gongdi ((?)???)
1 CONT Family name: Initially Yuan (?, yuán),
1 CONT later Tuoba (??, tuò bá)
1 CONT (changed 554)
1 CONT Given name: Kuo (?, kuò)
1 CONT Posthumous name: Gong (?, gong),
1 CONT literary meaning: "respectful"
1 CONT
1 CONT Emperor Gong of Western Wei ((?)???) (537-557), personal name né Yuan Kuo (??), later changed to Tuoba Kuo (???), was the last emperor of the Chinese/Xianbei state Western Wei -- a branch successor state to Northern Wei. He was ma
1 CONC de emperor in 554 after his older brother Emperor Fei was deposed by the paramount general Yuwen Tai. He carried little actual power, and in 556, after Yuwen Tai's death, Yuwen Tai's nephew Yuwen Hu, serving as guardian to Yuwen T
1 CONC ai's son Yuwen Jue, forced Emperor Gong to yield the throne to Yuwen Jue, ending Western Wei and starting Northern Zhou. The former emperor was killed in 557. Because Northern Wei's other branch successor state, Eastern Wei, had f
1 CONC allen in 551, Emperor Gong can be regarded as Northern Wei's final emperor as well.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Background
1 CONT     * 2 Reign
1 CONT     * 3 Death
1 CONT     * 4 Personal information
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Background
1 CONT
1 CONT Yuan Kuo was born in 537, as the fourth son of Emperor Wen. His mother's name is not recorded in history. In 548, Emperor Wen created him the Prince of Qi. However, nothing else is known about his activities during his father's re
1 CONC ign, during which the paramount general Yuwen Tai largely controlled power. Yuwen's hold on power appeared to be even firmer after Emperor Wen died in 551 and was succeeded by Yuan Kuo's older brother Yuan Qin (as Emperor Fei). Du
1 CONC ring Yuan Kuo's stint as the Prince of Qi, he married the daughter of the general Ruogan Hui (???) as his wife and princess.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 554, Emperor Fei, angry over Yuwen Tai's execution of the official Yuan Lie (??) in 553, plotted to have Yuwen killed. However, the news was leaked, and Yuwen deposed him. He selected Yuan Kuo to succeed Emperor Fei (as Emperor
1 CONC  Gong). Contemporaneously, Yuwen changed the imperial clan's surname from Yuan back to Tuoba, reversing the change that Emperor Xiaowen ordered in 496.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Reign
1 CONT
1 CONT Emperor Gong appeared to be even more powerless than his father and brother, and the power remained in Yuwen's hands. He created his wife, Princess Ruogan, empress. Later in 554, Yuwen poisoned the deposed Emperor Fei to death. La
1 CONC ter that year, an army dispatched by Yuwen and commanded by Yu Jin (??) captured rival Liang Dynasty's capital Jiangling (??, in modern Jingzhou, Hubei), capturing and executing Liang's Emperor Yuan. In spring 555, Emperor Gong cr
1 CONC eated Xiao Cha, a nephew of Emperor Yuan, the Emperor of Liang (as Emperor Xuan), but Xiao Cha was not recognized by most of Liang generals and officials, who instead recognized Xiao Yuanming, a candidate supported by Northern Qi,
1 CONC  as emperor.
1 CONT
1 CONT Later in 555, Yuwen required Tuoba Yu (???) the Prince of Huai'an to submit to Emperor Gong a propose to have the imperial princes reduced in rank to dukes, a proposal that Emperor Gong approved. In spring 556, pursuant to Yuwen's
1 CONC  desire to reorganize the governmental structure in accordance with the Zhou Dynasty governmental structure, the imperial government was reorganized into six departments.
1 CONT
1 CONT in fall 556, while Yuwen Tai was on a tour of the norther provinces, he became ill at Qiantun Mountain (???, in modern Guyuan, Ningxia). He summoned his nephew Yuwen Hu to Qiantun and entrusted the affairs of the state as well as 
1 CONC his sons to Yuwen Hu. He soon died, and Yuwen Tai's 14-year-old son Yuwen Jue took over his titles, while Yuwen Hu took the reigns of the state. Emperor Gong was subsequently forced to create Yuwen Jue the greater title of Duke of
1 CONC  Zhou.
1 CONT
1 CONT In spring 557, Yuwen Hu, believing that Yuwen Jue's youth meant that he needed to take imperial title to affirm his authority, forced Emperor Gong to yield the throne to Yuwen Jue, ending Western Wei and starting Northern Zhou.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Death
1 CONT
1 CONT Yuwen Jue (Emperor Xiaomin) initially created Tuoba Kuo the Duke of Song. However, less than two months after Tuoba Kuo yielded the throne, he was killed. His wife, the former Empress Ruogan, became a Buddhist nun.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Personal information
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Father
1 CONT           o Emperor Wen of Western Wei
1 CONT     * Wife
1 CONT           o Empress Ruogan (created 554)
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Emperor Fei of Western Wei Emperor of Northern Wei (Western)
1 CONT 554-556 Succeeded by
1 CONT None (dynasty destroyed)
1 CONT Emperor of China (Western)
1 CONT 554-556 Succeeded by
1 CONT Emperor Xiaomin of Northern Zhou
0 @N6000000002188195334@ NOTE Empress Feng (Wencheng)
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT
1 CONT Empress Feng (???, personal name unknown) (442-490), formally Empress Wenming (????, literally "the civil and understanding empress") was an empress of the Chinese/Xianbei dynasty Northern Wei. Her husband was Emperor Wencheng. Af
1 CONC ter her husband's death in 465, she overthrew the autocratic regent Yifu Hun in 466 and became regent over her stepson Emperor Xianwen and remained as such until his adulthood. She subsequently had a falling out with Emperor Xianw
1 CONC en (who had then become retired emperor) over his execution of her lover Li Yi (??), and she assassinated him and reassumed regency over his son Emperor Xiaowen in 476. While Emperor Xiaowen assumed imperial powers upon adulthood,
1 CONC  he remained very deferential to her, and she was highly influential until her death in 490.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 As empress
1 CONT     * 2 As regent for Emperor Xianwen
1 CONT     * 3 Between regencies
1 CONT     * 4 As regent for Emperor Xiaowen
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] As empress
1 CONT
1 CONT The later Empress Feng was born in 442. Her father Feng Lang (??) was the Duke of Xicheng and a provincial governor, and he was a son of Feng Hong, the last emperor of Northern Yan. Her mother was Lady Wang -- it is unclear whethe
1 CONC r Lady Wang was Feng Lang's wife or concubine. Feng Lang, along with his brothers Feng Chong (??) and Feng Miao (??), had surrendered to Northern Wei in 433, after believing that they were in danger of being killed by their stepmo
1 CONC ther, Feng Hong's wife Princess Murong. Later, Feng Lang was executed after being accused of crimes during his governorship. As Feng Lang's daughter, the later empress was seized to serve in the palace, but she was raised by her a
1 CONC unt, Emperor Taiwu's concubine Consort Feng. After Emperor Wencheng became emperor, she became his concubine in 455, carrying the rank of Guiren (??). In 456, she was created empress; this was probably after she completed, as acco
1 CONC rding to Northern Wei tradition, forging a golden statue, but there was no conclusive statement that she did so.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 465, Emperor Wencheng died. He was succeeded by his 11-year-old son Tuoba Hong the Crown Prince, and Empress Feng was honored as empress dowager. Two days later, as according to Northern Wei custom, Emperor Wencheng's personal 
1 CONC possessions were burned -- and while the ceremony was conducted, Empress Feng, in sadness, jumped into the fire. She was saved by the guards. Meanwhile, political power soon fell into the hands of the official Yifu Hun, who procee
1 CONC ded to execute many other key officials and effectively assumed regency.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] As regent for Emperor Xianwen
1 CONT
1 CONT In spring 466, however, Empress Dowager Feng staged a coup, probably in association with Tuoba Pi (???) and Jia Xiu (??), and Yifu Hun was arrested and executed. She assumed regency over the young Emperor Xianwen's regime, and she
1 CONC  engaged Jia, Gao Yun, and Gao Lü (??) as her assistants in the regency. Later, she also incorporated her brother Feng Xi (??) into the decision-making circle.
1 CONT
1 CONT Empress Dowager Feng was soon presented with a major opportunity to expand Northern Wei territory, as also in 466, rival Liu Song had a major dynastic succession struggle after Emperor Qianfei of Liu Song was assassinated in 465. 
1 CONC Emperor Qianfei's uncle Emperor Ming of Liu Song was declared emperor in the capital Jiankang, while his brother Liu Zixun the Prince of Jin'an was declared emperor in early 466 in Xunyang (??, in modern Jiujiang, Jiangxi). After 
1 CONC Emperor Ming's forces defeated Liu Zixun's and captured and executed Liu Zixun in fall 466, the Liu Song general Xue Andu (???), the governor of Xu Province (??, modern northern Jiangsu and northern Anhui), who had initially decla
1 CONC red for Liu Zixun, was apprehensive that he would be punished by Emperor Ming, and so decided to surrender to Northern Wei, and soon, he was joined by Bi Zhongjing (???) the governor of Yan Province (??, modern western Shandong) a
1 CONC nd Chang Zhenqi (???) the governor of Ru'nan Commandery (??, roughly modern Zhumadian, Henan). Empress Dowager Feng sent the general Weichi Yuan (???) to accept the surrenders of these Liu Song generals and to secure the region ju
1 CONC st north of the Huai River, and Weichi subsequently defeated two attempts by Emperor Ming to recapture those provinces. She also sent the general Murong Baiyao (????) to attack and try to capture Liu Song's Qing (??, modern centra
1 CONC l and eastern Shandong) and Ji (??, modern northwestern Shandong) Provinces, which were cut off from the rest of Liu Song after Xue's defection, and by 469, both provinces fell into Northern Wei hands, and all of the regions north
1 CONC  of the Huai River was by now Northern Wei territory.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 467, Emperor Xianwen's concubine Consort Li bore his oldest child Tuoba Hong, and Empress Dowager Feng personally raised the young prince. She soon terminated her regency and returned imperial powers to Emperor Xianwen, who was
1 CONC  13 years old at this point.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Between regencies
1 CONT
1 CONT While Empress Dowager Feng was no longer regent, she appeared to remain fairly influential during the reign of her stepson Emperor Xianwen. However, in 470, an event occurred that would damage their relationship. Empress Dowager F
1 CONC eng had taken the official Li Yi (??) as her lover. In 470, the official Li Xin (??), who was a close friend of Li Yi's brother Li Fu (??), was accused of corruption, and Emperor Xianwen became aware of the accusations even though
1 CONC  Li Fu tried to suppress the reports. He had known about his stepmother's relationship with Li Yi and, while he had not taken any actions against it at that point, disapproved of it. He sented Li Xin to death, but then informed Li
1 CONC  Xin that if he could report on crimes that Li Fu and Li Yi had committed, he would be spared. After initial reluctance, Li Xin did so, and another man named Fan Biao (??) also did so. Emperor Xianwen then executed Li Fu and Li Xi
1 CONC n. Empress Dowager Feng became resentful of Emperor Xianwen after that point.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 471, Emperor Xianwen yielded imperial title to his four-year-old son Tuoba Hong (who took the throne as Emperor Xiaowen), and he himself took the title of Taishang Huang (retired emperor). However, he continued to have actual p
1 CONC ower in the imperial government. In 476, still resentful of Emperor Xianwen, Empress Dowager Feng killed him. (Most historians, including Sima Guang, believed that she poisoned him, but another version indicated that Empress Dowag
1 CONC er Feng readied assassins who, when Emperor Xianwen came to her palace to greet her, seized and smothered him.) Empress Dowager Feng took on the title of grand empress dowager and reassumed regency, over the nine-year-old Emperor 
1 CONC Xiaowen.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] As regent for Emperor Xiaowen
1 CONT
1 CONT After Grand Empress Dowager Feng reassumed regency, she was said to be more dictatorial than she was before, but intelligent in her decisions and frugal in her living. Not only was she highly literate, but she also was capable in 
1 CONC mathematics. However, she trusted several eunuchs and permitted them to interfere in governmental matters. Further, she greatly promoted her lovers Wang Rui (??) and Li Chong (??) -- both of whom were apparently talented officials
1 CONC , but whose promotions were beyond what their talents and contributions called for. She balanced her reputation by also promoting some honored officials who were not her lovers. Because she was concerned that she would be criticiz
1 CONC ed for what was seen as immoral conduct, she punished those whom she perceived to be criticizing her or parodying her behavior with severe punishment, including death. One of her victims was Li Xin, who had contributed to her prio
1 CONC r lover Li Yi's death, as she had Li Xin put to death in 477. Fearful that Emperor Xiaowen's mother's clan would try to take power, she falsely accused his grandfather Li Hui (??) the Prince of Nan Commandery of treason in 478 and
1 CONC  had him and his clan slaughtered. She apparently accelerated the policy of Sinicization, which included social stratification, as she issued an edict in 478 requiring people to marry in their social classes.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 479, after rival Liu Song's throne was usurped by the general Xiao Daocheng, who established Southern Qi as its Emperor Gao, Northern Wei commissioned Liu Chang (??) the Prince of Danyang, a Liu Song prince who had fled to Nort
1 CONC hern Wei in 465, with an army and promising him support to rebuild Liu Song. However, Liu Chang's abilities were not up to task, and he was never able to gain much following in the border regions to mount a major drive to reestabl
1 CONC ish Liu Song. By 481, the campaign had fizzled.
1 CONT
1 CONT Also in 481, the Buddhist monk Faxiu (??) tried to start a popular uprising at the capital Pingcheng (??, in modern Datong, Shanxi), but was discovered, captured, and executed. Some officials advocating the execution of all Buddhi
1 CONC st monks, but Grand Empress Dowager Feng refused. Also that year, she started the building of her future tomb at Fang Mountain (??), near Pingcheng, leaving instructions that after she died that it would be unnecessary for her to 
1 CONC be buried with her husband Emperor Wengcheng (who was buried near the old Northern Wei capital Shengle (??, in modern Hohhot, Inner Mongolia). Later that year, a new criminal code that she commissioned Gao Lü to write was complete
1 CONC d -- with 832 sections, 16 of them prescribing clan-slaughter as penalty, 235 of them prescribing personal death penalty, and 377 prescribing other forms of punishment.
1 CONT
1 CONT As the years went by, as Emperor Xiaowen grew in age, he appeared to gradually assume more and more power. Sometime during the process, Grand Empress Dowager Feng apparently became apprehensive of his abilities and therefore had h
1 CONC im detained and considered deposing him in favor of his brother Tuoba Xi (???), but after her attendants persuaded her otherwise, she did not carry out such actions. While Grand Empress Dowager Feng never formally returned imperia
1 CONC l powers to him, by about 483 he appeared to be fairly in control of the government, although Grand Empress Dowager Feng continued to retain substantial powers. Indeed, it was by her order that that year, after Emperor Xiaowen's c
1 CONC oncubine Consort Lin bore his oldest son, Tuoba Xun, that Consort Lin was forced to commit suicide pursuant to Northern Wei customs. She raised Tuoba Xun herself. In 485, after Emperor Xiaowen created his younger brothers princes,
1 CONC  Grand Empress Dowager Feng established an imperial school for these princes. In 486, perhaps as both a sign of Sinicization and demonstration of Emperor Xiaowen's authority, he began to assume traditional Chinese imperial clothin
1 CONC g, including a robe with dragon patterns and a tassled hat.
1 CONT
1 CONT The power-sharing arrangement between stepgrandmother and stepgrandson could perhaps be illustrated by an incident in 489, when Emperor Wencheng's younger brothers Tuoba Tianci (????) the Prince of Ruyin and Tuoba Zhen (???) the P
1 CONC rince of Nan'an were accused of corruption, a death offense. Grand Empress Dowager Feng and Emperor Xiaowen jointly convened an imperial council to discuss their punishment. Grand Empress Dowager Feng opened by asking the official
1 CONC s, "Do you believe that we should care about familial relations and destroy law, or to disregard familial relations and follow the law?" The officials largely pleaded for the princes' lives. After Grand Empress Dowager Feng fell s
1 CONC ilent, Emperor Xiaowen stated: "What the two princes committed is unpardonable, but the Grand Empress Dowager takes after the brotherly love that Gaozong [Emperor Wengcheng's temple name] had. Further, the Prince of Nan'an is fili
1 CONC ally pious toward his mother. Therefore, the two will be spared the death penalty, but their offices and titles will be stripped from them, and they will be reduced to commoner status with no political rights."
1 CONT
1 CONT In 490, Grand Empress Dowager Feng died, and she was buried with magnificent honors. Emperor Xiaowen was so distraught that he was unable to take in food or water for five days, and subsequently observed a three-year mourning peri
1 CONC od for her, notwithstanding officials' pleas for him to shorten the mourning period in accordance with rules that Emperor Wen of Han had set.
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Empress Helian Empress of Northern Wei
1 CONT 456-465 Succeeded by
1 CONT Empress Feng Qing
0 @N6000000002188195465@ NOTE Consort Yujiulü
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Consort Yujiulü (?????, personal name unknown) (d. 452), formally Empress Gong (???, literally "the respectful empress"), was a consort of Tuoba Huang (Crown Prince Jingmu), a crown prince of the Chinese/Xianbei dynasty Northern W
1 CONC ei. She was the mother of Emperor Wencheng.
1 CONT
1 CONT Consort Yujiulü was a sister of Yujiulü Pi (????), who was a member of Rouran's royal house, but who surrendered to Northern Wei and thereafter became a Northern Wei general, during Emperor Taiwu's reign. Consort Yujiulü herself w
1 CONC as described as beautiful, and during Emperor Taiwu's reign she was selected to be a consort for his son Tuoba Huang. She was much favored, and she bore him his oldest son Tuoba Jun in 440. Her husband died in 451, however, after 
1 CONC becoming ill over fear of false accusations by the eunuch Zong Ai. Zong subsequently assassinated Emperor Taiwu and Tuoba Huang's younger brother Tuoba Yu (whom he had briefly made emperor) in 452, and then was overthrown by high-
1 CONC ranking officials, who made Tuoba Jun emperor (as Emperor Wencheng). Consort Yujiulü died soon thereafter without having been honored as an empress dowager, but later that year, Emperor Wencheng did posthumously honor his parents 
1 CONC as emperor and empress, and she became known as Empress Gong.
0 @N6000000002188195476@ NOTE Tuoba Huang
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Tuoba Huang (???) (428-451), formally Crown Prince Jingmu (????) (literally "the decisive and solemn crown prince"), later further formally honored as Emperor Jingmu (????) with the temple name Gongzong (??) by his son Emperor Wen
1 CONC cheng, was a crown prince of the Chinese/Xianbei dynasty Northern Wei. He was the oldest son of Emperor Taiwu, and was created crown prince in 432 at the age of four, and as he grew older, Emperor Taiwu transferred more and more a
1 CONC uthority to him. However, in 451, he incurred the wrath of his father due to false accusations of the eunuch Zong Ai, and many of his associates were put to death. He himself grew ill in fear, and died that year.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Early life
1 CONT     * 2 After Emperor Taiwu's partial transfer of authority
1 CONT     * 3 Death and aftermaths
1 CONT     * 4 Personal information
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Early life
1 CONT
1 CONT Tuoba Huang was born in 428, as Emperor Taiwu's oldest son. His mother's name was render as Consort He (???), but since both the Helan (??) and Helai (??) clans later changed their names to He (during the reign of Tuoba Huang's gr
1 CONC eat-grandson Emperor Xiaowen), it is unclear whether her name was Helan or Helai. She died the year that she gave birth to him, suggesting that she might have died in childbirth, but there is no conclusive evidence that it happene
1 CONC d.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 432, Emperor Taiwu created Tuoba Huang crown prince, at the same time that he created one of his consorts, Consort Helian, empress. He became a highly ranked official in his father's administration that same year, although, at 
1 CONC age four, the position was likely nominal. In 433, Emperor Taiwu tried to negotiate a marriage between Crown Prince Huang and one of the daughters of Emperor Wen of Liu Song, but Emperor Wen, while not immediately rejecting the pr
1 CONC oposal, did not agree either.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 439, when Emperor Taiwu was on a campaign to conquer Northern Liang, he had Crown Prince Huang assume imperial authority at the capital Pingcheng (??, in modern Datong, Shanxi), assisted by the high-ranking official Qiumuling S
1 CONC hou (????), to guard against a Rouran attack. However, Qiumuling, not believing that Rouran would actually attack, took little actual precautions, and when Rouran's Chilian Khan Yujiulü Wuti attacked, Pingcheng was caught nearly d
1 CONC efenseless. Qiumuling wanted to escort Crown Prince Huang to the hills south of Pingcheng and take up defense position there, but at the opposition of Emperor Taiwu's wet nurse Empress Dowager Dou, Crown Prince Huang remained in P
1 CONC ingcheng, and when Northern Wei forces subsequently defeated Rouran forces, Yujiulü Wuti was forced to withdraw. (By this point, although he was only 11, Crown Prince Huang was apparently already participating in major military an
1 CONC d policy decisions, as he had opposed his father's Northern Liang campaign, but was overruled by his father, who trusted Cui Hao's advice that Northern Liang was easily defeatable.)
1 CONT
1 CONT By 442, Crown Prince Huang appeared to be already a devout Buddhist, and when his father, at the suggestion of Cui and the Taoist monk Kou Qianzhi, built the very high and difficult-to-construct Jinglun Palace (???), he tried to o
1 CONC ppose on account of cost, but Emperor Taiwu did not agree.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 443, Crown Prince Huang accompanied his father on a campaign against Rouran, and when theysuddenly encountered Yujiulü Wuti, Crown Prince Huang advised an immediate attack, but Emperor Taiwu hesitated, allowing Yujiulü Wuti to 
1 CONC escape. From that point on, Emperor Taiwu began to listen to Crown Prince Huang's advice in earnest, and in winter 443, he authorized Crown Prince Huang to carry out all imperial duties except the most important ones, under assist
1 CONC ance from Qiumuling, Cui, Zhang Li (??), and Tuxi Bi (???). Crown Prince Huang soon instituted a policy to encourage farming -- by mandatorily requiring those who had extra cattle to loan them to those without, to be animals of bu
1 CONC rden, with the lease being paid for by those without cattle by tilling the grounds of the cattle owners, increasing the efficiency of the farmlands greatly.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] After Emperor Taiwu's partial transfer of authority
1 CONT
1 CONT In 446, while Emperor Taiwu was attacking the Xiongnu rebel Gai Wu (??), he found a large number of weapons in Buddhist temples in Chang'an. Believing that the monks were aligned with Gai, he slaughtered the monks in Chang'an. Cui
1 CONC  Hao used this opportunity to encourage Emperor Taiwu to slaughter all monks throughout the empire and destroy the temples, statues, and sutras, and notwithstanding Kou Qianzhi's opposition, Emperor Taiwu proceeded to slaughter th
1 CONC e monks in Chang'an, destroy the statues, and burn the sutras. He then issued an empire-wide prohibition of Buddhism. Crown Prince Huang, however, used delaying tactics in promulgating the edict, allowing Buddhists to flee or hide
1 CONC , but it was said that not a single Buddhist temple remained standing in Northern Wei. This was the first of the Three Disasters of Wu.
1 CONT
1 CONT By 450, Crown Prince Huang appeared to be in direct conflict with Cui over administration of the state. When Cui recommended a number of men to be commandery governors, Crown Prince Huang objected strenously, and yet the men were 
1 CONC commissioned over his objection at Cui's insistence. It appeared that Crown Prince Huang had a hand when, later in 450, Cui was put to death with his entire clan, on account of having defamed imperial ancestors, as Crown Prince Hu
1 CONC ang argued hard to spare one of Cui's staff members, Gao Yun, and during that process, Gao gave some statements regarding Cui that could be viewed either as exculpatory or inculpatory.
1 CONT
1 CONT In fall of 450, when Liu Song's Emperor Wen sent his general Wang Xuanmo (???) to attack Huatai (??, in modern Anyang, Henan), Emperor Taiwu personally led an army to relieve Huatai, and subsequently, after defeating Wang, advance
1 CONC d deeply into Liu Song territory, all the way to the Yangtze River. During Emperor Taiwu's campaign against Liu Song, pursuant to his instructions, Crown Prince Huang was on the northern border, defending against a potential Roura
1 CONC n attack.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Death and aftermaths
1 CONT
1 CONT Crown Prince Huang was described to be high observant, but trusting of his associates and also spending much effort on managing orchards and farms, to earn money from them. Gao Yun tried to advise him against engaging in commercia
1 CONC l ventures and in overly delegating authorities, but he did not listen. Rather, in 451, he became embroiled in a conflict with the eunuch Zong Ai, whose corruption he had found out and whom he disliked immensely. Zong, apprehensiv
1 CONC e that Crown Prince Huang's trusted associates Chouni Daosheng (????) and Ren Pingcheng (???) would accuse him of crimes, acted preemptorily and accused Chouni and Ren of crimes. In anger, Emperor Taiwu executed Chouni and Ren, an
1 CONC d many other associates of Crown Prince Huang were entangled in the case and executed as well. In fear, Crown Prince Huang became ill and died.
1 CONT
1 CONT Emperor Taiwu soon became aware that Crown Prince Huang was not involved in any crimes, and he regretted his actions greatly. Around the new year 451, he created Crown Prince Huang's oldest son Tuoba Jun the Prince of Gaoyang, but
1 CONC  soon removed that title on the account that the crown prince's oldest son should not be a mere imperial prince -- a strong sign that he was intending to create Tuoba Jun crown prince ventually. Zong, in fear that Emperor Taiwu wo
1 CONC uld punish him, assassinated Emperor Taiwu in spring 452, and then seized power, killing a number of officials and Crown Prince Huang's younger brother Tuoba Han (???) the Prince of Dongping, while making another younger brother o
1 CONC f Crown Prince Huang's, Tuoba Yu the Prince of Nan'an emperor. Zong controlled the imperial regime, and when Tuoba Yu tried to assert his own power in fall 452, Zong assassinated him as well. Officials led by Yuan He, Baba Kehou (
1 CONC ????), Dugu Ni (???), and Buliugu Li rose against Zong and killed him, making Tuoba Jun emperor, and Tuoba Jun, after he took the throne as Emperor Wencheng, posthumously honored Tuoba Huang as an emperor.
1 CONT
