0 @N6000000002188101196@ NOTE Emperor Gaozu of Tang
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Emperor Gaozu of Tang 
1 CONT Birth and death: 566–Jun. 25, 635 
1 CONT Family name: Li (?) 
1 CONT Given name: Yuan (?) 
1 CONT Courtesy name (?): Shude (??) 
1 CONT Dates of reign: Jun. 18, 618¹–Sept. 4, 626² 
1 CONT Dynasty: Tang (?) 
1 CONT Temple name: Gaozu (??) 
1 CONT Posthumous name:
1 CONT
1 CONT (short) Emperor Shenyao³ (????) 
1 CONT Posthumous name:
1 CONT
1 CONT (full) Emperor Shenyao Dasheng
1 CONT Daguang Xiao4
1 CONT ????????? 
1 CONT General note: Dates given here are in the Julian calendar.
1 CONT They are not in the proleptic Gregorian calendar. 
1 CONT ——— 
1 CONT 1. Was already in control of Chang'an and de facto master of China
1 CONT since December 12, 617 
1 CONT 2. Abdicated in favor of his son, and was granted the title
1 CONT Taishang Huang (???), a title reserved for retired emperors. 
1 CONT 3. Given in 674 
1 CONT 4. Given in 754 
1 CONT Emperor Gaozu of Tang China (566 - June 25, 635), born Li Yuan, was the founder of the Tang Dynasty of China, and the first emperor of this dynasty from 618 to 626. Under the shortlived Sui dynasty, Li Yuan was the governor in the
1 CONC  area of modern-day Shanxi province, and was based in Taiyuan, Shanxi.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Establishment of the Tang Dynasty
1 CONT In 615, Li Yuan was assigned to garrison Lunghsi. He gained much experience by dealing with the Gokturks of the north and was able to pacify them. Li Yuan was also able to gather support from these successes and, with the disinter
1 CONC gration of the Sui dynasty in July of 617, Li Yuan - urged on by his second son Li Shimin (later Taizong emperor) - rose in rebellion. Using the title of "Great Chancellor" (???), Li Yuan installed a puppet child emperor, Emperor 
1 CONC Gong, but eventually removed him altogether and established the Tang Dynasty in 618.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Reign as Emperor
1 CONT Li Yuan's reign was concentrated on uniting the empire under the Tang. Aided by his son, Li Shimin, he defeated all the other contenders, including Li Gui, Dou Jiande, Wang Shichong, Xue Ju and Liu Wuzhou. By 628, the Tang Dynasty
1 CONC  had succeeded in uniting all of China. On the homefront, Li Yuan recognized the early successes forged by the Sui dynasty and strived to emulate most of its policies, including the equal distribution of land amongst its people, a
1 CONC nd he also lowered taxes. He abandoned the harsh system of law set about during the Sui dynasty as well as reforming the judicial system. These acts of reform paved the way for the reign of Emperor Taizong, which ultimately pushed
1 CONC  Tang China to the height of its power.
1 CONT
1 CONT Li Yuan abdicated in 626 in favour of his son, Taizong emperor after the Xuan Wu Gate incident (?????) when Taizong had to kill his two immoral brothers who were attempting to usurp the throne. Li Yuan lived on as "Grand Emperor" 
1 CONC (Taishang Huang) until his death in 635.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT (none) Emperor of the Tang Dynasty Succeeded by:
1 CONT Taizong 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Gong of Sui Emperor of China 
0 @N6000000002188195980@ NOTE Tuoba Fu
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Tuoba Fu (Chinese: ???; pinyin: Tuòbá Fú) (?-294), chieftain of the Tuoba (293-294). He is the son of Tuoba Shamohan (?????) and the brother of Tuoba Yituo and Tuoba Yilu. In 293, he succeeds Tuoba Chuo as the chieftain of the Tuo
1 CONC ba. His predecessor is his father's younger brother. Upon his death in 294, he is succeeded by Tuoba Luguan, another one of his uncles.
0 @N6000000002188198036@ NOTE Princess Duan (Wenming)
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Princess Duan (???, personal name unknown) was the wife of the Chinese/Xianbei state Former Yan founder Murong Huang. She was likely a daughter of a Duan tribal chief, as the Duans and the Murongs intermarried frequently. When Mur
1 CONC ong Huang claimed the title "Prince of Yan" in 337, she was created princess. There was no further mentioning of her during the reign of Murong Huang or his son and successor Murong Jun, who was likely her son, but there is no con
1 CONC clusive evidence. It is not known when she died. Sometime after Murong Jun declared himself emperor in 352, she was honored as an empress, although it was not clear whether she was still alive at that point. After Former Yan's fal
1 CONC l in 370 and subsequent "restoration" as Later Yan in 384 by Murong Chui, the son of her husband Murong Huang but by his concubine Consort Lan, not her, Murong Chui excluded her from her husband's temple and enshrined his mother C
1 CONC onsort Lan instead.
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT None (dynasty founded) Princess of Former Yan
1 CONT 337-348? Succeeded by
1 CONT Empress Kezuhun
0 @N6000000002188198025@ NOTE Murong Hui
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Murong Hui (???), (269—333) Xianbei chief and Duke Xiang of Liaodong, posthumously honored as Prince Wuxuan of Yan.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Murong Hui had initially been a Xianbei chief who fought Jin forces during the late reign of Emperor Wu of Jin, Jin's founding emperor, but he submitted as a Jin vassal in 289. Under constant attack by fellow Xianbei chief Duan Ji
1 CONC e (??) of the Duan tribe, he humbly sought peace with the Duan and married one of Duan Jie's daughters. From this union came Murong Huang (in 297) and two of his younger brothers, Murong Ren (???) and Murong Zhao (???).
1 CONT
1 CONT During Murong Hui's rule as tribal chief, the Jin Dynasty's central government was in constant turmoil and eventually collapsed due to infighting and agrarian rebellions, the strongest of which was the Xiongnu state Han Zhao. As a
1 CONC  result, many refugees arrived in the relatively safe domain of Murong Hui's, and as he treated the ethnically Han refugees with kindness, most chose to stay, greatly strengthening his power, and as Jin forces in the north gradual
1 CONC ly fell to Han Zhao's capable general Shi Le (who later established his own independent state Later Zhao), Murong Hui became the only domain in northern China still under titular Jin rule, carrying the Jin-bestowed title of the Du
1 CONC ke of Liaodong. He entrusted Murong Huang with many important military tasks such as fighting the powerful fellow Xianbei Yuwen tribe in 320 and 325. In early 322, Murong Hui named Murong Huang heir apparent. However, Murong Hui a
1 CONC lso greatly favored Murong Huang's brothers Murong Ren, Murong Zhao, and particularly Murong Han (???), who was regarded very highly as a general. Murong Huang became jealous and suspicious of these brothers, feelings which became
1 CONC  known to them and which would sow the seeds of future troubles.
